    "q-103108": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103108",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$1-\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(4\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=$",
      "worked_solution": "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) & =1-\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(4\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =1-\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(4\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-103108-a",
